@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix pmw:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/works/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — Theravāda Buddhism
#
# The tradition with the closest thing to a graded phenomenology of the
# bodily rapture the modern draft calls a wave, and with the strongest
# counterweight: it catalogues the capacities and forbids their display in
# the same corpus.
#

pmt:Theravada a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Theravāda"@en, "Theravāda"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Pāli canon and its commentarial literature, of which the Visuddhimagga of Buddhaghosa is the systematising handbook."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. The Visuddhimagga citations name the translation they were read from, and Dīgha Nikāya 2 was reopened for this purpose. For Majjhima Nikāya 118, Dīgha Nikāya 11 and Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8 the edition or translation is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed; those three passages are to be reopened."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Pali Canon, in the Dīgha and Majjhima Nikāyas, the Vinaya and the Jātaka; Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya, pmw:MajjhimaNikaya, pmw:VinayaPitaka, pmw:Jataka ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#
# Added on 2026-09-21 with the first passage from outside the Visuddhimagga
# group. The file was not migrated before; AGENTS.md requires the migration of
# anyone who touches a tradition file, and it is the cheap half of this change.
#

pmw:Visuddhimagga a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga"@en, "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga"@de ;
    skos:note "The systematising handbook of the tradition and the source of most of this file. Read in Ñāṇamoli's English of 1956."@en .

pmw:DighaNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Dīgha Nikāya"@en, "Dīgha Nikāya"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at suttas 2 and 11. The edition of the second is not recoverable and prima-materia#45 records that."@en .

pmw:MajjhimaNikaya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Majjhima Nikāya"@en, "Majjhima Nikāya"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at sutta 118. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:VinayaPitaka a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Vinaya Piṭaka"@en, "Vinaya Piṭaka"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at Cūḷavagga V.8. The edition is not recoverable; see prima-materia#45."@en .

pmw:Jataka a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Jātaka"@en, "Jātaka"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-09-21 at story 49 only, in the English of Robert Chalmers under E. B. Cowell's editorship, volume I of 1895. The remaining five hundred and forty-six stories were not read. All six volumes are public domain and reachable by the regular route, which makes this the cheapest continuation for anything the canon states as doctrine and the Jātaka reports as a case."@en .

#
# Practices
#

pmp:Anapanasati a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:MajjhimaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Ānāpānasati"@en, "Ānāpānasati"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mindfulness of in-breath and out-breath, set out as a sixteen-step progression in the discourse and as a meditation subject in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Majjhima Nikāya 118", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:AnapanasatiYieldsPiti a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Mindfulness of breathing yields rapture"@en, "Atemachtsamkeit bringt Verzückung hervor"@de ;
    skos:note "Rapture appears in the handbook as a factor of absorption reached through the meditation subject, not as something aimed at directly. The pairing is recorded at that strength."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:Piti ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmp:BrahmaviharaBhavana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Cultivation of the divine abidings"@en, "Kultivierung der göttlichen Verweilungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The deliberate raising and sustaining of a chosen affective state as a meditation subject in its own right."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IX (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Capacities and states
#

pmc:Piti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Pīti"@en, "Pīti"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rapture as a bodily felt state, graded in the handbook into five degrees of increasing pervasion."@en ;
    skos:note "The five degrees are khuddikā, khaṇikā, okkantikā, ubbegā and pharaṇā."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:KhuddikaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Minor rapture"@en, "Geringe Verzückung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The least of the five degrees of rapture, described as able to raise the hairs of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the passage that attests piloerection as a recognised concomitant of a meditative state, not as its object."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:UbbegaPiti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Uplifting rapture"@en, "Erhebungsverzückung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fourth of the five degrees of rapture, described as strong enough to lift the body."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Manomayakaya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Mind-made body"@en, "Geistgemachter Körper"@de ;
    skos:definition "A second body, formed and complete in its faculties, said to be produced from this one; the classical form of the claim the modern draft calls exteriorisation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Dibbacakkhu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Divine eye"@en, "Göttliches Auge"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sight beyond the range of the physical eye."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Dibbasota a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Divine ear"@en, "Göttliches Ohr"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hearing beyond the range of the physical ear."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Cetopariyanana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge encompassing another mind"@en, "Kenntnis fremden Bewusstseins"@de ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the state of another consciousness."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Steps a prerequisite claim points at
#

pmc:Sila a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Virtue"@en, "Sittlichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first of the three trainings, on which the handbook is built as a commentary on the verse about the tangle."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Samadhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration"@en, "Sammlung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second of the three trainings, named in the handbook as the condition for the six direct knowledges."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:FourthJhana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The fourth absorption"@en, "Die vierte Vertiefung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The absorption from which the handbook has the supernormal powers proceed, and which it calls their natural plane."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:Iddhividha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The kinds of supernormal power"@en, "Die Arten übernatürlicher Kraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Transformation by supernormal power, the first of the direct knowledges treated in the handbook."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmp:FourteenfoldMastery a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Control of the mind in fourteen ways"@en, "Beherrschung des Geistes in vierzehn Weisen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A numbered course of mastery over the meditation objects, among them the order of the kasiṇa and its reverse, skipping the absorption, skipping the object, and the definition of the object."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.2 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Prerequisite claims
#
# The strongest passage in the whole collection stands here. The handbook
# does not merely order the training; it says outright that the attempt
# cannot even be begun without what precedes it.
#

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourteenfoldMastery a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Supernormal power presupposes the fourteenfold mastery"@en, "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierzehnfache Beherrschung voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:FourteenfoldMastery ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that it is not possible for a meditator even to begin to accomplish transformation by supernormal power without having first completed the development of control of the mind in those fourteen ways." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:IddhiPresupposesFourthJhana a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Supernormal power presupposes the fourth absorption"@en, "Übernatürliche Kraft setzt die vierte Vertiefung voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The handbook assigns the task to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption the natural plane for obtaining the power. It does not, at these places, say what happens to one who attempts it from elsewhere."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.12-13 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:DirectKnowledgesPresupposeConcentration a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The direct knowledges presuppose concentration"@en, "Die höheren Kenntnisse setzen die Sammlung voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Dibbacakkhu, pmc:Dibbasota, pmc:Cetopariyanana, pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that the six kinds of direct knowledge are arrived at with the support of perfected concentration and by nothing besides that." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration presupposes virtue"@en, "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Sila ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:FiveHindrances a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The five hindrances"@en, "Die fünf Hemmnisse"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt, whose abandoning the discourse describes immediately before entry into the first absorption."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" .

pmc:FourAbsorptions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The four absorptions"@en, "Die vier Vertiefungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A graded series in which each stage is reached by letting go of a factor still present in the one before it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:JhanaPresupposesAbandoningTheHindrances a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Absorption presupposes the abandoning of the hindrances"@en, "Die Vertiefung setzt das Aufgeben der Hemmnisse voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FiveHindrances ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The discourse places the abandoning of the five immediately before entry into the first absorption in a fixed formula, without stating at that place what befalls one who enters otherwise."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" .

pmc:AbsorptionsAreGraded a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "Each absorption presupposes the one before it"@en, "Jede Vertiefung setzt die vorhergehende voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "The series is strictly graded in both canon and handbook, each stage defined by what it has let go of. No passage was found that forbids skipping in so many words, which is why this is recorded as an order and not as a necessity."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:SixteenStepsOfBreathing a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:MajjhimaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing"@en, "Die sechzehn Schritte der Atemachtsamkeit"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    skos:note "Recorded at the weakest strength on purpose. The discourse sets out four tetrads of four steps and assigns them to the four foundations of mindfulness. It presents a progression; it does not say that a step is unreachable without the one before it, and the question whether the order is binding or expository is contested among practitioners to this day."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Majjhima Nikāya 118" .

#
# A dispute
#

pmc:AbsorptionThresholdDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga, pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "How high the threshold of absorption stands"@en, "Wie hoch die Schwelle der Vertiefung liegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what is required for absorption. The discourses describe it as reached in the ordinary course of the training; the handbook sets a far higher bar, remarking that only one in a hundred or a thousand accomplishes it."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement bears directly on every prerequisite in this file that runs through the fourth absorption. If the handbook's threshold is the right one, the capacities it makes conditional on that absorption are conditional on something very few reach; if the discourses' is, they are not."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Cautions
#
# Both are load-bearing for any project that collects capacity claims: one
# refuses the display of powers as a means of persuasion, the other names
# the states of practice that are mistaken for its completion.
#

pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya, pmw:VinayaPitaka ;
    rdfs:label "Display of powers refused"@en, "Zurschaustellung der Kräfte verweigert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The discourse distinguishes three marvels, of psychic power, of mind-reading and of instruction, and holds only instruction to be reliable; the display of power for the sake of persuasion is refused as something to be ashamed of."@en ;
    skos:note "The Vinaya rule against displaying superhuman states to laypeople stands alongside it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Dibbacakkhu, pmc:Dibbasota, pmc:Cetopariyanana, pmc:Manomayakaya ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 11 (Kevaṭṭa Sutta)", "Vinaya, Cūḷavagga V.8" .

pmc:ImperfectionsOfInsight a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The imperfections of insight"@en, "Die Unvollkommenheiten der Einsicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ten states arising in practice, among them luminosity, rapture, tranquillity, pleasure and resolve, which are not defilements in themselves but become obstacles when taken as proof of attainment."@en ;
    skos:note "The handbook illustrates the error with the monk who held himself awakened for sixty years. This is the tradition's own answer to the question of how a practitioner mistakes a state for a result."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Piti ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XX.105ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# Placement on the awareness axes. Compiler acts: the handbook says "from the
# fourth absorption", it does not say "withdrawn absorption". The coordinate
# is ours and is entered as ours.
#

pmc:IddhiIsExercisedInWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The supernormal powers are exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en, "Die übernatürlichen Kräfte werden in abgezogener Vertiefung ausgeübt"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbook assigns the powers to a meditator who has reached the fourth absorption and calls that absorption their natural plane. The absorption is one of the four described as awake and turned from the senses to a single object, which is the criterion this coordinate names. The placement is of the exercise, not of the training that leads to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII.49 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:ManomayakayaIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DighaNikaya ;
    rdfs:label "The mind-made body is a displaced locus"@en, "Der geistgemachte Körper ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Manomayakaya ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situationGround "The source has a second body, complete in its faculties, produced from this one and going where this one does not. That is the criterion for the displaced value; whether anything of the sort occurs is a separate question and not this one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta, DN i.77 (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" .

pmc:SamadhiIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en, "Die Sammlung liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbook names concentration as the condition of the direct knowledges and develops it as the four absorptions, which it describes as awake and turned from the senses to a single object. The coordinate is read off that development and not off the word itself; concentration in a weaker sense would not carry it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

#
# The day-choosing refused, and refused with a case
#

pmc:TheravadaNakkhatta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Jataka ;
    rdfs:label "The fool may watch for lucky days, and luck shall always miss"@en, "Der Tor mag auf günstige Tage achten, und das Glück wird ihm immer entgehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The forty-ninth story, told twice, once of the present and once of the past, and both times as a run with an outcome. A family has fixed a wedding day and afterwards asks its household ascetic whether the stars are favourable for it. The ascetic, piqued at having been consulted second, answers that they are not and that great misfortune would follow; the family stays at home; the other side, having prepared everything, marries the girl to somebody else; and the party that obeyed the constellation goes home with nothing. A wise man passing through puts the argument in one stanza: the fool may watch for lucky days, yet luck shall always miss; it is luck itself that is luck's own star, and what can mere stars achieve."@en ;
    skos:note "What makes this worth a node rather than a proverb is that the refusal is carried by a case and not by a doctrine. The transmission does not argue that constellations have no power; it reports an occasion on which the rule was followed exactly and the follower lost the thing he wanted, and it names the mechanism of the loss — an offended interpreter with a motive. The same corpus that supplies this puts nakṣatra prognostication among the low arts elsewhere, which is the doctrinal form; this is the other form, and it is the one a protocol can use."@en ;
    skos:note "The motive matters and is stated in the text twice. The ascetic knows the stars are indifferent and says otherwise because he was asked second. A transmission that explains a false reading of the calendar by the reader's pique has located the failure in a party — which is exactly the location the holding's generalization about calendar-bound operations says these transmissions do not use. It does not refute that statement, because this is not a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar; it is one that reports somebody else's binding. The difference is recorded here so that a later run does not count it on the wrong side."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Jātaka 49, Nakkhatta-Jātaka, Fausbøll i.257-259, in the English of Robert Chalmers, ed. E. B. Cowell, The Jātaka or Stories of the Buddha's Former Births, vol. I, Cambridge University Press 1895, pp. 125-126" .
